Keeps the textures a background effect let go, and gives them to the
next effect of their size, rather than making them again in the frame
that shows it. smithay's damage tracker keeps a framebuffer effect's
cache only for the elements a frame draws, so a layer surface whose
blur region goes empty, as a shut launcher's does while it stays
mapped, lost its framebuffer and blur textures, and each opening made
all five inside niri's render: 22 to 48 ms of it on a 4K screen under
load. Blur already remade textures only on a size change; that and a
framebuffer of a new size now hand theirs to the pool too. Each
renderer's context keeps its own pool, oldest let go past 64 MB, and a
texture still shared is never kept, since the blur's output stays
drawn from until its intermediate goes. At debug, each frame a blur's
textures change, it says its size, how many it made and took, and how
long that took on the CPU. It adds no setting.

